These extra guards only complicate the code where it is not needed anyway. The `Drop` function checks the number of `items` in a table before dropping elements (see below), so that dropping the uninitialize table as well as table with no actual data (but with `FULL` control bytes) is safe . Added tests to check that the current behavior of `Drop` won't change in the future.

```rust
impl<T, A: Allocator + Clone> Drop for RawTable<T, A> {
    fn drop(&mut self) {
        if !self.table.is_empty_singleton() {
            unsafe {
                self.drop_elements();
                self.free_buckets();
            }
        }
    }
}

impl<T, A: Allocator + Clone> RawTable<T, A> {
    unsafe fn drop_elements(&mut self) {
        if Self::DATA_NEEDS_DROP && !self.is_empty() {
            for item in self.iter() {
                item.drop();
            }
        }
    }
}
```

The Draggable plugin from the jsMind library was not available in the studio due to [RequireJS](https://requirejs.org/) not being able to load the plugin's script. This issue was fixed by reverting to the previous method of loading using [$.getScript](https://api.jquery.com/jquery.getscript/).

**Demo:**

![fix-draggable](https://github.com/eduNEXT/xblock-mindmap/assets/134975835/7fb48da5-5545-4b4f-8c9c-4242ea5e7566)


#### What is RequireJS?
RequireJS is a library that enables the import and export of modules in JavaScript.

Here's an example of how RequireJS works:
```
<script type="text/javascript" src="https://requirejs.org/docs/release/2.3.6/comments/require.js"></script>
<script type="text/javascript">
  // Load modules
  require.config({
    paths: {
      jquery: "https://code.jquery.com/jquery-3.7.0.min"
    }
  });
  
  // Use a module
  require(["jquery"], function (jquery) {  
    // You can use jquery  here to refer to the loaded jQuery module
    console.log('jquery', jquery);
  });
 </script>
```

#### What was the problem here?
The problem arose from the fact that the jsMind draggable module was exported in a way that RequireJS was unable to load it. Here's the problematic code:

```
<div id="jsmind_container"></div>
<script type="text/javascript" src="https://requirejs.org/docs/release/2.3.6/comments/require.js"></script>
<script type="text/javascript">
  require.config({
    paths: {
      jsMind: "https://unpkg.com/jsmind@latest/es6/jsmind",
      draggable_node:
        "https://jsd.onmicrosoft.cn/npm/jsmind@0.6.4/js/jsmind.draggable-node",
      // other paths if needed
    },
  });

  require(["jsMind", "draggable_node"], function (jsMind, draggableNode) {
    // Load jsMind and draggable_node
    console.log(draggableNode); // this gets undefined
  });
</script>
```
This is the reason why it wasn't functioning properly. The issue was resolved by using [$.getScript](https://api.jquery.com/jquery.getscript/) to dynamically fetch the script.



